WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.725
Logging should support diagnosis without
exposing more file system detail than

2
00:00:04.805 --> 00:00:05.675
operators need.

3
00:00:05.755 --> 00:00:09.981
A log may need to identify that a path was
rejected or that a file operation failed,

4
00:00:10.061 --> 00:00:13.789
but it should not casually disclose
sensitive filenames, tenant data,

5
00:00:13.869 --> 00:00:16.914
internal directory layouts, or private
storage details.

6
00:00:16.994 --> 00:00:20.846
The diagnostic value should be balanced
against the data being revealed.

7
00:00:20.926 --> 00:00:23.536
Testing should exercise the defensive
boundary.

8
00:00:23.616 --> 00:00:26.288
Useful tests include empty names, very
long names,

9
00:00:26.368 --> 00:00:29.786
unexpected separators, absolute-style
names, reserved names,

10
00:00:29.866 --> 00:00:33.470
unusual but allowed characters,
platform-specific case behavior,

11
00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:36.160
link-related cases in controlled
environments,

12
00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:39.657
and names that are valid for one workflow
but invalid for another.

13
00:00:39.737 --> 00:00:42.533
The point is to confirm the allowlist and
root policy,

14
00:00:42.613 --> 00:00:44.788
not to publish reusable misuse material.

15
00:00:44.868 --> 00:00:48.659
Platform differences should be documented
where they affect behavior.

16
00:00:48.739 --> 00:00:52.032
Separators, case sensitivity, link
semantics, permissions,

17
00:00:52.112 --> 00:00:55.592
path length limits, reserved names, and
rename behavior can vary.

18
00:00:55.672 --> 00:00:59.649
A course or codebase that pretends every
platform behaves the same will miss

19
00:00:59.729 --> 00:01:01.282
important review questions.

20
00:01:01.362 --> 00:01:04.655
Static analysis and compiler warnings can
support the review.

21
00:01:04.735 --> 00:01:07.283
They may identify unchecked string
operations,

22
00:01:07.363 --> 00:01:10.781
path buffers with risky length handling,
ignored return values,

23
00:01:10.861 --> 00:01:15.086
and control flow that reaches file
operations without passing through expected

24
00:01:15.166 --> 00:01:16.285
validation helpers.

25
00:01:16.365 --> 00:01:20.342
These signals are most useful when the
code structure makes policy visible.

26
00:01:20.422 --> 00:01:24.212
Review should trace every path from input
through validation, joining,

27
00:01:24.292 --> 00:01:28.766
resolution, authorization, open, write,
rename, delete, extraction, and cleanup.

28
00:01:28.846 --> 00:01:32.885
The reviewer should ask which root is
approved, which component controls it,

29
00:01:32.965 --> 00:01:36.134
what name policy applies, how the final
location is checked,

30
00:01:36.214 --> 00:01:38.202
and what happens when each step fails.

31
00:01:38.282 --> 00:01:41.700
That trace is the practical test of
traversal-resistant design.
